\section{Treat Every Expression as a Stream}

Classic Lucid gives expressions sequence-valued meanings. Constants denote constant streams, ordinary arithmetic is pointwise, \texttt{next} removes the first element, \texttt{first} repeats the first element as appropriate to its use, and \texttt{fby} constructs a stream from an initial value and a tail. Equations describe relationships among whole streams; textual order is not assignment order \cite{ashcroft1977lucid,wadge1985lucid}.

For streams \(A\) and \(B\), \(A\ \texttt{fby}\ B\) has \(A_0\) at index zero and \(B_{i-1}\) at positive index \(i\). This shift makes a guarded recurrence productive. Without the initial value, an equation such as \(x=x+1\) does not become an imperative increment; it is an inconsistent pointwise equation over ordinary integers.

\section{Define a Running Sum by Equations}

A Lucid program can return the stream \texttt{sum}:

\begin{verbatim}
sum
where
  sum = 0 fby sum + input;
end
\end{verbatim}

For input \(\langle3,5,10,\ldots\rangle\), the result begins \(\langle0,3,8,18,\ldots\rangle\). At index \(i>0\), the \texttt{fby} tail asks for \(\texttt{sum}_{i-1}+\texttt{input}_{i-1}\). Whether an implementation pushes tokens through filters or educes requested elements is not fixed by these equations. \Cref{fig:visual-358} makes the one-step dependency explicit.

