\section{Calculate One Requested Element}

\begin{verbatim}
value(sum, 0) = 0
value(sum, i) where i > 0:
    previous := value(sum, i - 1)
    item := value(input, i - 1)
    return previous + item
\end{verbatim}

Memoization avoids recomputing the prefix, but memoization is an implementation strategy. The stream equation defines the same prefix even for a correct token-driven implementation.

\section{Lower Equations With Index Meaning Intact}

A Lucid IR can use nodes for pointwise operators, \texttt{first}, \texttt{next}, and \texttt{fby}; dependency edges annotated with index shifts; explicit initial state for realizable delays; stream element types and partiality/effect information; a logical index clock; and source provenance for equations and operator occurrences. Compilation may produce an eductive graph and warehouse keys, a lazy stream program, or a stateful loop that emits successive elements.

Legal optimizations include pointwise fusion, constant propagation, and elimination of duplicate pure demands. Retiming is legal only when initial values and observed indices are preserved. Semantic-preservation risks include turning a demand for one element into eager traversal of an infinite stream, moving a partial operation before a selector, and applying ordinary algebraic rewrites to floating-point streams. Compare requested stream prefixes, including errors or nontermination where the language observes them.

\section{Make the Boundary at Index Zero Visible}

For a stream \(X=\langle3,5,10,\ldots\rangle\), compare three operators applied independently to \(X\). The table shows only the terms that follow from the supplied prefix; a dash is not a source-level error but an unspecified input beyond the fixture. Notice that \texttt{first} repeats the value at index zero; it is not a prefix-taking operation that shortens the stream.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\hline
Expression & Index 0 & Index 1 & Index 2 & Index 3 \\
\hline
\(X\) & 3 & 5 & 10 & --- \\
\texttt{first X} & 3 & 3 & 3 & 3 \\
\texttt{next X} & 5 & 10 & --- & --- \\
\texttt{0 fby X} & 0 & 3 & 5 & 10 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

An evaluator can model these operators as demand rewrites. The dispatch below expands a demand for an expression at an index into requests for operands at potentially different indices. Treat input lookup as a partial operation so the harness can detect an unnecessary request outside its prefix. A demand for \texttt{0 fby X} at zero does not require any inspection of \(X\).

\begin{verbatim}
at(node, i):
    require i >= 0
    match node:
        Constant(k): return k
        Input(name): return request_input(name, i)
        First(x): return at(x, 0)
        Next(x): return at(x, i + 1)
        Fby(seed, tail):
            if i == 0: return at(seed, 0)
            return at(tail, i - 1)
        Add(left, right):
            return at(left, i) + at(right, i)
\end{verbatim}

For the running sum, request index 3 first. Expansion takes the tail three times and reaches the initialized result after demanding exactly the first three input positions. Request index 1 next; a warehouse can reuse that result rather than reread input. To see why the initialization matters even under optimization, compare \texttt{next (0 fby X)} with \texttt{0 fby next X}. The former equals \(X\); the latter starts at zero and then gives \(X_1,X_2,\ldots\). Interchanging the operators would shift the visible frontier of demand and change the first result. A useful fixture makes every input component distinct so an off-by-one shift cannot masquerade as an arithmetic error.

\paragraph{Distinguish a run-time frontier from a stream equation.}
An evaluator serving index 3 must sometimes wait for input 2, whereas a
streaming loop is normally called only when that input has arrived. Give
both the same trace of available input, not the same host call sequence.
Record \texttt{sum[0]} immediately, then deliver one input at a time and
compare the resulting prefix after each delivery. If a client asks for
\texttt{sum[2]} before input 1 arrives, the evaluator should not publish an
invented value to fill that slot. Waiting, rejecting the request, and
reporting a definedness error are different boundary policies. Adopt one
that is consistent with how the host supplies the input stream.
